WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.975
Now the big ones.

2
00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:06.175
Tropical cyclones are sorted by their sustained wind.

3
00:00:06.425 --> 00:00:09.750
A tropical depression: under thirty-four knots.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.650
A tropical storm: thirty-four to sixty-three knots.

5
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.850
That’s when it gets a name.

6
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.300
A hurricane: sixty-four knots and over.

7
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:25.075
And a major hurricane is Category three or higher: ninety-six knots and above.

8
00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:28.150
Notice thirty-four and sixty-four again.

9
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.775
They’re the same numbers as the gale and hurricane force warnings.

10
00:00:33.025 --> 00:00:39.800
The Atlantic season runs from June first through November thirtieth, and it peaks from August through October.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.300
Two more words to keep straight.

12
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.675
A hurricane watch, and a hurricane warning.

13
00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:48.025
Which one is more urgent?

14
00:00:49.025 --> 00:00:50.550
The warning.

15
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.650
A watch means conditions are possible, usually within forty-eight hours.

16
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.075
A warning means they’re expected, usually within thirty-six.
